{
 "id": "latency",
 "question": "How does latency break down in a voice pipeline?",
 "voice": "meher",
 "model": "lightning_v3.1_pro",
 "sample_rate": 24000,
 "durationSec": 33.225,
 "narration": "Latency is a stack, not one number. The client round trip sits outside the model and depends on region. Pulse streaming time to first token is about 150 milliseconds at one concurrency, and about 300 milliseconds when many streams run together. Lightning time to first byte is about 200 milliseconds measured in region, in Mumbai or Oregon. A distant laptop often measures 500 to 800 milliseconds because the round trip dominates synthesis. On phone audio, send 8000 hertz PCM instead of upsampling.",
